// @no-instantiate
// TODO(miscompile): a TOP-LEVEL inferred empty `[]` whose element a `.push` pins to an ARRAY
// checks clean and emits a module that does not instantiate.
//
// The directive is BARE deliberately. The two engines that run this corpus word the same
// rejection differently and share no substring to match on — wasmtime says `type mismatch:
// expected i32, found (ref $type)` and V8 says `expected type i32, found global.get of type
// (ref 1)`. Pinning either text would make the file fail on the other host, so it pins the
// FACT (this module does not instantiate) and leaves the wording to the engine.
//
// The IN-FUNCTION form of this program is fixed and pinned at
// `tests/cases/arrays/nested-array-inferred-empty-push.vl`. The fix lives in `collectLocals`,
// which walks a FUNCTION BODY's block statements to type wasm LOCALS; a top-level binding is a
// GLOBAL and never reaches it, so the synthesis that closes the hole inside a function does not
// run out here.
//
// WHY THIS IS PINNED RATHER THAN FIXED. Extending the synthesis to globals is a different
// mechanism, not a wider guard — and the top-level path is not uniformly broken, which is what
// makes the residue narrow enough to pin honestly. The other element kinds at top level REJECT
// cleanly rather than miscompiling:
//
//   * a STRUCT element   -> `emitProgram: field access receiver is not a struct`
//   * a UNION element    -> `emitProgram: `is` receiver is not a union value`
//   * a CLOSURE element  -> `emitProgram: callee is not a function name`
//
// A MAP element is unverified: probing it hits an unrelated checker limit (`print of a union
// value (i32 | null) … not yet supported by codegen`) before the list is ever reached, so it is
// evidence in neither direction and is listed as unknown rather than folded into the claim.
//
// A clean rejection is safe; this one is not, and that asymmetry is the whole reason this file
// exists. When the global path grows the same synthesis, flip this to `@run` + `@log 3` and move
// it beside its in-function sibling — that is the trip `xfail-miscompile-permuted-struct-fields`
// made when its own ruling reached the emitter.
const outer = []
const inner = [1, 2, 3]
outer.push(inner)
print(outer[0][2])
